$OctSeventeenArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'craft-beer-can-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Craft Beer Can Labels: Plan the Seam, Condensation and Small Runs'
    Description = 'A practical buyer guide to craft beer can labels: measure the can, protect the seam, trial condensation, manage seasonal SKUs and approve the finished pack.'
    Category = 'Beverage packaging'
    CardDescription = 'A label that fits a bottle proof may fight a can seam or wrinkle on a chilled shelf.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated craft beer can labels with complete hop and citrus artwork, applied to unbranded cans beside a wrap proof'
    Caption = 'Check the wrap on a filled, chilled can before approving the production dieline.'
    Published = '2026-10-17'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can I use my bottle label artwork on a can?', 'The visual system can carry across, but a can needs its own usable width, height and seam proof. Do not reuse the bottle dieline without measuring a finished can.'),
      @('Do craft beer can labels need a waterproof material?', 'Define actual condensation, ice-bucket and handling exposure first. Trial the proposed face material, print protection and adhesive on filled cans.'),
      @('How should seasonal flavors be controlled?', 'Keep an approved SKU list covering artwork, product name, code and quantity, then verify the first finished can at each changeover.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A craft beer can can look excellent on a flat artwork proof and awkward once the label is wrapped. The seam lands beside a flavor name, the bottom edge drifts toward the curved shoulder, and a cold can leaves the front panel wet before anyone takes a shelf photo. I would start with a filled can and a paper wrap, not the screen.</p>
  <h2>Measure the Can That Will Be Labeled</h2>
  <p>Record the straight-wall height and usable circumference of the actual can. Leave clearance from the top and bottom curves. Mark the intended overlap or gap on a full-size paper prototype. If the brand changes can supplier or can size, repeat this step. A generic template may be close enough to look plausible but still put important words at the joint.</p>
  <p>Place the complete original artwork where a shopper can see it: a hop illustration, a citrus motif and a strong product name can share the front without forcing the style name into the seam. Reserve readable space for owner-approved product information and any later-applied code. The beverage brand must supply and approve market-specific copy; a label supplier should not invent it.</p>
  <h2>Test the Cold, Wet Route</h2>
  <p>Ask when labeling happens. A dry can on a packing line is a different application surface from a cold can pulled from storage. Trial the proposed construction at the actual application stage. Chill filled samples, handle them with damp hands and inspect edges, print rub and the joint. If the cans sit in ice water, include that exposure instead of calling ordinary condensation an ice-bucket test.</p>
  <p>Small runs often mean many versions. Put can size, beer name, artwork revision, code field, quantity and pack-out order on one approved sheet. Check the first labeled can after every artwork or roll change. A pretty seasonal label is expensive if the wrong flavor reaches the wrong carton.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send a filled can and its dimensions</li><li>Mark the seam and front viewing zone</li><li>Trial the true application temperature</li><li>Test condensation and any ice exposure separately</li><li>Approve each SKU and code field</li><li>Check a finished can at every changeover</li></ul>
  <p>Our <a href="/blog/custom-craft-beer-labels-guide/">craft beer bottle label guide</a> covers the broader beverage brief. The <a href="/blog/custom-roll-labels-for-bottles-and-jars/">roll label guide</a> helps with application format. Send the can, artwork and variant list through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote form</a>.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'small-sample-vial-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Small Sample Vial Labels: Keep the Name Readable on a Tiny Curve'
    Description = 'Plan labels for cosmetic and fragrance sample vials with tiny type, curved glass or plastic, cap clearance, variant control and physical proofing.'
    Category = 'Sample packaging'
    CardDescription = 'The vial is small; the decision about what must be readable cannot be postponed.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of several small unbranded fragrance sample vials with complete original botanical labels and distinct scent names beside a final-size paper proof'
    Caption = 'Read the label on the filled vial, not only enlarged on a monitor.'
    Published = '2026-10-17'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a full bottle label be scaled down for a vial?', 'Usually that makes the type and details too small. Rebuild the hierarchy around the vial and owner-approved information.'),
      @('Should the label cross the cap seam?', 'Only if that placement has a defined purpose and is tested on the real closure. A front identity label often works better below the cap.'),
      @('Who approves cosmetic sample text?', 'The responsible brand or distributor must approve market-specific ingredients, warnings and claims. The label supplier can flag fit and readability problems.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A fragrance sample vial is the package people are most likely to hold close to their eyes. It is also the package where a designer may try to shrink a full bottle label to one quarter of its size. I would not approve that shortcut. The illustration can survive; the text hierarchy usually cannot.</p>
  <h2>Start With the Filled Vial and Closure</h2>
  <p>Measure the straight printable area below the cap, including the radius of any shoulder. Wrap a plain paper strip around a finished vial to locate the joint and see whether the label covers the product view. A rollerball, spray vial and tiny screw-cap bottle each leave a different usable panel. Ask whether the vial is glass or plastic and how the sample will be packed with other vials.</p>
  <p>Keep a complete original botanical or geometric mark, but give the scent or product name priority. If several scents share a design family, use a second cue besides color so a person sorting samples can distinguish them in a dim stockroom. Do not let the seam hide the only variant name.</p>
  <h2>Proof Actual-Size Type and Handling</h2>
  <p>Print the longest approved name at final size. Apply it to a filled vial, place the cap, then read it in ordinary light. If the back panel needs more owner-approved copy than the vial can carry legibly, the brand must decide how the outer pack and vial share information. The printer should not compress legal or ingredient wording until it is technically present but unusable.</p>
  <p>Check whether fragrance oil, water, or repeated fingers touch the label during use. Trial the proposed material and print protection under the intended handling route. The word waterproof does not tell you how an adhesive behaves on a narrow curve or whether a small edge starts to lift inside a sample kit.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Provide filled vials with final caps</li><li>Measure the straight printable area</li><li>Identify the front and label joint</li><li>Print the longest name at actual size</li><li>Check handling and product contact</li><li>Approve every scent and pack version</li></ul>
  <p>Compare the <a href="/blog/custom-essential-oil-labels-guide/">small bottle label guide</a> and the <a href="/blog/white-ink-small-text-custom-stickers/">small-type proofing guide</a>. A vial photo and approved copy are useful starting points for the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-tea-tin-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Tea Tin Labels: Make the Lid, Body and Refill Work Together'
    Description = 'Plan custom labels for tea tins around curved metal, lid alignment, flavor identification, refill use and version control.'
    Category = 'Tea packaging'
    CardDescription = 'A tin has two visible surfaces and often a second life after the first tea is gone.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of unbranded metal tea tins with complete original leaf illustrations and distinct tea names on lid and body labels beside loose tea'
    Caption = 'Check lid and body labels together on a closed, filled tin.'
    Published = '2026-10-17'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should the product name go on the lid or the tin body?', 'Use the real display and storage position to decide. If tins stack, the lid matters; if they face outward on a shelf, the body must identify the tea.'),
      @('Can a tea tin label be changed for refills?', 'That depends on the brand workflow and chosen construction. Define whether a permanent decorative panel stays and a variable identity label changes.'),
      @('Can one label size fit every tea tin?', 'Only after measuring each tin and checking curvature, seams and lid clearance on actual samples.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A tea tin looks orderly in a row until someone stacks the tins. Then only the lids are visible. Move them to a shelf and the body becomes the selling face. I would ask how the tins are stored before deciding which panel carries the tea name. The lid and body do not need identical artwork, but they do need to agree.</p>
  <h2>Map the Two Viewing Positions</h2>
  <p>Put finished tins in the intended display: stacked, side by side or inside a gift box. Mark the available flat lid area and straight body height. Check whether a rolled edge, closure ridge or vertical metal seam interrupts the label. A round lid proof should sit centered after the lid is fully closed, not only when it is loose on a table.</p>
  <p>Use complete original leaf or botanical illustrations to build a family, then make the tea type readable in more than color alone. A worker picking two similarly green tins needs a clear word, not an almost invisible hue difference. If the tin is a gift, check that any ribbon or sleeve does not cover the only product identity.</p>
  <h2>Plan for the Next Fill</h2>
  <p>Some tins are sold once; others are meant to be refilled. If the latter, decide what remains permanent and what must change with the contents. A decorative brand label could stay on the body while an owner-approved variant and batch label changes on the lid. Trial removal and relabeling on the final coated metal. Do not promise clean removal from a material name alone.</p>
  <p>Check the label after normal hand handling, lid opening and any expected kitchen moisture. The food business must approve product description, ingredients and market-specific wording. The supplier can help choose space and legibility, but should not invent a tea claim to fill the panel.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Photograph the intended shelf and stack</li><li>Measure lid and straight body areas</li><li>Check seams, rolled edges and closure clearance</li><li>Proof all variants on closed tins</li><li>Define keep-or-replace for refill use</li><li>Verify approved copy by tea type</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-tea-packaging-labels-guide/">tea packaging guide</a> covers wider format choices. For reuse, compare the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable and permanent label guide</a>. Send tin dimensions and the display photo through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote form</a>.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'labels-for-textured-plastic-pails'
    Title = 'Labels for Textured Plastic Pails: Test the Surface Before Printing'
    Description = 'Plan industrial pail labels around textured plastic, curved walls, handling, variable data and actual-container adhesion trials.'
    Category = 'Industrial packaging'
    CardDescription = 'The label can be beautifully printed and still lift from a pebbled bucket wall.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of unbranded textured plastic pails with complete original geometric product labels and clear batch panels in a small industrial packing area'
    Caption = 'Test a finished label on the exact pail wall and handling route.'
    Published = '2026-10-17'
    Faq = @(
      @('Will a stronger adhesive always fix a textured pail?', 'Not necessarily. Resin, texture, surface treatment, contamination and application pressure all matter. Trial the full label construction on the actual pail.'),
      @('Can the label cover a molded ridge?', 'A ridge changes contact and may lift an edge. Measure a flatter application zone and test the final size before crossing it.'),
      @('Who approves chemical or safety information?', 'The product owner must supply and approve the correct market-specific content. The printer should not invent classifications, warnings or compliance claims.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A textured plastic pail can make a label look secure for five minutes and loose by the time it reaches storage. The wall is curved, the molded surface is uneven, and someone may grab it by a handle while the label brushes against another bucket. I would ask for the actual pail before choosing a face stock or adhesive.</p>
  <h2>Find the Real Application Zone</h2>
  <p>Mark the flattest uninterrupted area on a filled container. Measure the available height below any top rim and above a lower ridge. Note molded marks, ribs and the handle swing. Ask when the pail is labeled: before filling, after filling or after the exterior has been wiped. Dust, oil or residue can change the result even when the plastic resin is unchanged.</p>
  <p>Trial final-size labels, not tiny sample dots, on production pails. Apply them with the same pressure and method that the line will use. Check edges after the normal hold, stacking and transport routine. If the pail will be outdoors, wet or exposed to contents, define those exposures separately and test them. No generic adhesive name guarantees a result on every textured wall.</p>
  <h2>Keep Identification Legible in a Stack</h2>
  <p>An original geometric color system can help identify product families, but the owner-approved product name and batch field must remain readable from the working angle. Keep variable-data panels away from the strongest curve. If a barcode is used, scan it on the labeled pail with the intended scanner after stacking, not only on a flat proof sheet.</p>
  <p>For regulated or hazardous contents, the product owner has to supply and approve current market-specific wording and symbols. A supplier can flag a space problem; it cannot decide classification or make a safety claim from a photograph.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Supply the exact pail resin and finished sample</li><li>Mark ridges, handles and application zone</li><li>Describe cleaning and labeling sequence</li><li>Trial final-size construction on real pails</li><li>Inspect after stacking and handling</li><li>Approve all product and variable fields</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">adhesive and surface guide</a> and the <a href="/blog/thermal-transfer-printable-labels-guide/">variable-data overprint guide</a>. Send pail photos, contents and handling conditions through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'warehouse-shelf-bin-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Warehouse Shelf and Bin Labels: Design for the Picker, Not the Proof'
    Description = 'Plan warehouse shelf and bin labels for reading distance, scan angle, location changes, surface preparation and controlled reprints.'
    Category = 'Warehouse identification'
    CardDescription = 'A tidy label file means little if a picker cannot find the right bin at speed.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of warehouse shelf bins with complete original high-contrast location labels, readable codes and color markers, with a worker checking a scan'
    Caption = 'Judge the label from the position where the picker actually stands.'
    Published = '2026-10-17'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should shelf labels and bin labels carry the same code?', 'Use the warehouse system as the source of truth. Decide whether a code identifies a shelf position, a movable bin or both, then print and inspect accordingly.'),
      @('How large should a location code be?', 'Choose size from the actual viewing distance, lighting and mounting angle. Proof the longest real code at finished size in the aisle.'),
      @('Can old labels simply be covered?', 'Only after checking scan interference, edge build-up and obsolete information. A clean removal or opaque correction plan may be safer.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>In a warehouse, the useful label is the one a picker can read before pulling the wrong bin. A proof viewed at a desk hides the aisle distance, the rack shadow and the angle of the scanner. I would walk the intended route with a full-size printout and ask where the person's eyes land.</p>
  <h2>Decide What the Code Identifies</h2>
  <p>A fixed shelf location is not the same thing as a movable tote or product SKU. Write down which record the printed code points to in the warehouse system. If bins move but locations do not, the two may need separate labels. The warehouse owner must supply approved IDs and the scan destination; a printer should not invent a location scheme because the sample code looks neat.</p>
  <p>Proof the longest actual location ID in the aisle. Check reading from standing distance and from a step stool where relevant. Place a barcode where the intended scanner can reach without twisting around a beam. Test a final print on the rack and in the lighting of the operating shift.</p>
  <h2>Choose Surface and Changeover Together</h2>
  <p>Painted steel, powder-coated shelves and textured plastic bins may each need a different construction. Clean a representative area using the normal site process, then trial the label with real handling. If locations are revised regularly, plan removal, overlay or replaceable holders before buying a permanent roll. Stacking old codes under a new sticker can confuse both people and scanners.</p>
  <p>Original high-contrast arrows or color blocks can make zones easier to navigate, but color should never be the only way to tell two locations apart. Keep a human-readable ID beside the code. On reprints, compare the approved file version to the system export and retire obsolete labels before the next shift uses them.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Separate location, bin and product identifiers</li><li>Supply real code data from the warehouse system</li><li>Proof at aisle distance and scan angle</li><li>Trial each rack and bin surface</li><li>Define removal and changeover procedure</li><li>Check first print against approved IDs</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/returnable-tote-identification-labels-guide/">returnable tote guide</a> addresses movable containers; the <a href="/blog/shipping-carton-identification-labels-guide/">carton ID guide</a> covers outbound packs. Share shelf photos and the location export through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote request</a>.</p>
'@
  }
)
